  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nSaturday, The ridge actually looks to retrograde a bit, though this \nwill change little for the Ozarks besides ensure that it's not \nmoving on anytime soon. Winds get very light through the weekend as \nsurface high pressure passes over, but maximized height rises and \nclear skies will ensure that daytime highs still approach or exceed \n100 degrees. Heat Index values look to be highest Saturday, with the \nentire area experiencing HI values between 100 and 110.\n\nA weak backdoor cold front move through the state throughout \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. The better supporting energy \nlooks to stay to the east, however some ensembles are outputting \nvery splotchy areas of light rain. This is not a scenario I would \nput much stock in as our deliverance from the dry spell, but there \nis currently a 10-15% chance to see a few raindrops Saturday, which \nis more than we have had in a while. It does look to bring a \n*slight* cooldown to our far east/south-central Missouri for Monday \nwhere highs will be in the low 90s...but this won't involve much of \nour CWA, and will be short-lived.\n\nThe global models indicate that Fujiwhara-ing low pressure centers \nejecting out of the northwest midweek will flatten the ridge as they \nmove by to the north and send a surface cold front south that \nwill be approaching the area by Thursday morning. Though this \nscenario is uncertain as we begin talking about 8+ days out from\nnow, it is perhaps the first glimmer of hope that we may at \nleast temporarily see temperatures decrease by a smidge. \nHowever, the CPC 8-14 day hazards outlook does indicate that we \nstill have a slight risk (20- 40%) for Extreme Heat September \n11-17.",
